        "timestamp": 1784339438
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>oh <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Set/Card.html#Set.encard_eq_coe_toFinset_card\">Set.encard_eq_coe_toFinset_card</a> does it, thanks <span aria-label=\"pray\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f64f\" role=\"img\" title=\"pray\">:pray:</span></p>",
        "id": 611397440,
        "sender_full_name": "Nathan",
        "timestamp": 1784339671
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>here's the full code <a href=\"https://github.com/axiomizer/lean/blob/main/MathlibProj/misc/askmath.lean\">https://github.com/axiomizer/lean/blob/main/MathlibProj/misc/askmath.lean</a></p>",
        "id": 611397988,
        "sender_full_name": "Nathan",
        "timestamp": 1784340260
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Instead of using a sum of binomial coefficients you can do a bijection proof by saying that the number of subsets is obviously 32 as you make 5 random choices for whether the first five elements are in and then there's always a unique thing to do with the 6th element to make the total even.</p>",
        "id": 611407933,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784350008
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>oh yeah, then i could just get the cardinality of the powerset of Fin 5, and i wouldn't have to partition the set into a bunch of pieces</p>",
        "id": 611408503,
        "sender_full_name": "Nathan",
        "timestamp": 1784350627
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You'd still have to prove that the map I describe is a bijection (and in particular write down the maps in both directions) but this sounded easier than the 0,2,4,6 approach which was why I mentioned it</p>",
        "id": 611409885,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1784351350
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The map (from <code>Set (Fin n+1)</code> to itself) should be an involution (switch the status of 0 between \"in the set\" and \"not in the set\"), so that proving it is a bijection should be a breeze. It should also be easy to prove it maps sets of [even|odd] cardinality to sets of [odd|even] cardinality, and that should do the trick.</p>\n<p>(I'm saying this without trying to do it in Lean, but as a plan it seems fine)</p>",
        "id": 611454548,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1784396721
    }
]